---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {CHROMATIC} from '../../lib/music.js';
---
<BaseLayout title="Piano Chord Finder by Notes | Free Interactive Tool" description="Choose 3 or 4 piano notes to identify major, minor, suspended, diminished, augmented, seventh and half-diminished chords. Works in your browser." canonicalPath="/tools/chord-finder/" crumbs={[{name:'Chord Finder',href:'/tools/chord-finder/'}]}>
<div class="container page-hero tool-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Free interactive tool</span><h1>Chord finder<br/>by piano notes.</h1><p class="lead">Know the notes but not the chord name? Choose three or four pitch classes. We'll match all ten chord families in the 120-chord library and show alternative names when the same piano keys admit different interpretations.</p></div>
<section class="container section-compact"><div class="two-col"><div class="panel tool-panel"><h2>Select your notes</h2><p>Choose a set of 3 or 4 distinct pitch classes. Octaves don't change the basic chord identification.</p><div class="toggle-grid" id="pitch-picker">{CHROMATIC.map(p=><button class="pitch-toggle" type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-pitch={p.pc}>{p.label}</button>)}</div><label class="input-label" for="finder-bass">Lowest note / bass (optional)</label>
  <select class="field" id="finder-bass" aria-describedby="finder-bass-help">
    <option value="">Not specified — identify by notes only</option>
    {CHROMATIC.map(p=><option value={p.pc}>{p.label}</option>)}
  </select>
  <p class="help-text" id="finder-bass-help">Choose the physically lowest note if known. A chord tone gives its inversion (for example C/E); a different bass note produces a slash-chord interpretation, not an inversion.</p>
  <div class="button-row no-print"><button class="btn" type="button" id="clear-pitches">Clear selection</button><button class="btn" type="button" id="example-pitches">Try C–E–G</button></div><p class="help-text">Chord names can be ambiguous without a bass note, key and harmonic context.</p></div><aside class="panel tool-panel"><h2>Possible matches</h2><div id="finder-results" class="results-box" aria-live="polite">Select three or four notes to see matching chords.</div><p class="help-text" style="margin-top:12px">A chord may share its sound with an enharmonic spelling; our results explain that distinction instead of pretending there is always one name.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="container section-compact prose"><h2>How the chord finder works</h2><p>The finder compares selected pitch classes against ten formulas: major, minor, diminished, augmented, suspended second, suspended fourth, dominant seventh, major seventh, minor seventh and half-diminished seventh. It uses twelve-tone equal temperament and matches pitch classes, not the musical key. The optional bass selector lets you name an inversion such as C/E when E is the lowest note; a non-chord bass is marked as a separate slash-bass interpretation rather than a false inversion. Several names can still be valid. Continue to the <a class="text-link" href="/tools/inversion-explorer/">120-chord inversion explorer</a> to hear the actual voicings.</p></section>
<section class="container section-compact">
  <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">LEARN BY EXAMPLE</span><h2>Identify piano chords from the notes you have</h2><p>Start with three notes, check the results, and use the sound and bass note to choose the right interpretation.</p></div></div>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <article class="panel"><h3>C–E–G: C major</h3><p>Select the white keys C, E and G. They form a major triad because E is four semitones above C and G is seven semitones above C. The same three pitch classes can appear as E–G–C or G–C–E when the chord is inverted.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/chords/c-major/">Open C major notes, audio and inversions ↗</a></p></article>
    <article class="panel"><h3>A–C–E: A minor</h3><p>Choose A, C and E. The interval from A to C is a minor third (three semitones), which distinguishes A minor from A major (A–C♯–E). A minor and C major share two notes, but they are different chords.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/chords/a-minor/">Learn A minor on piano ↗</a></p></article>
    <article class="panel"><h3>C–E–G–B♭: C7</h3><p>Adding B♭ to C major makes a dominant seventh chord, C7. If the top note is B natural instead, the chord is Cmaj7. That one-semitone difference changes the seventh quality.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/seventh-chords-piano/">Compare C7, Cmaj7 and minor sevenths ↗</a></p></article>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="container section-compact">
  <div class="two-col">
    <article class="panel prose"><h2>Why can a chord have more than one name?</h2><p>This finder matches <em>pitch classes</em>: C4 and C5 both count as C. It uses the bass you specify, but does not hear the physical octave or decide which chord function a composer intended. Some note sets can legitimately match several chord names or enharmonic spellings. Do not assume that the first match is the only correct one.</p><p>If you know which note is lowest, visit the <a href="/tools/inversion-explorer/">inversion explorer</a> to identify the chord's bass and compare its voicings. To hear how a chord changes into another, use the <a href="/tools/chord-compare/">side-by-side comparison tool</a>.</p></article>
    <article class="panel prose"><h2>What if your chord is not recognised?</h2><p>The tool currently supports ten families of three- and four-note chords. A five-note extended chord, a chord missing its root, or a note cluster outside those formulas may not return a match. That is a scope limit of this tool, not proof that the notes cannot be musical.</p><p>Try entering only the chord tones you know, or use the <a href="/tools/virtual-piano/">88-key virtual piano</a> to hear the complete voicing. You can also <a href="/chords/">browse the 29 authored lessons</a> for worked note spellings.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
